  <owl:Class rdf:about="http://davidkoepsell.com/cfr404#GeneralizedTonicClonicSeizures">
    <rdfs:label>generalized tonic-clonic seizures</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/BFO_0000031"/>
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://davidkoepsell.com/cfr404#InstitutionalCriterion"/>
    <cfr:sourceSection>App1-111.00</cfr:sourceSection>
    <cfr:chunkId>App1-111.00</cfr:chunkId>
    <cfr:sourceQuote>e brain. There are various types of generalized and “focal” or partial seizures. In children, the most common potentially disabling seizure types are generalized tonic-clonic seizures, dyscognitive seizures (formerly complex partial seizures), and absence seizures. However, psychogenic nonepileptic</cfr:sourceQuote>
